   (:eng "The invariant is what lets IQR compare distributions with different units by scaling." :math "Quantiles are linear in their argument under positive scaling." :cs "Runnable; single boolean confirms."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "stat/iqr computes Q3-Q1."
    :eng-composition "(define (outlier-detector data)\n  ;; Chain stat/quartile and stat/iqr to detect outliers via Tukey fences.\n  (let* ((q1 (stat/quartile data 1))\n         (q3 (stat/quartile data 3))\n         (iqr (stat/iqr data))\n         (lo (- q1 (* 1.5 iqr)))\n         (hi (+ q3 (* 1.5 iqr))))\n    (filter (lambda (x) (or (< x lo) (> x hi))) data)))\n(outlier-detector '(10 12 11 15 14 13 16 17 18 100))"
    :math-composition "(define (modified-z-score data)\n  ;; Robust z-score using median and IQR instead of mean and SD.\n  (let ((med (stat/median data))\n        (iqr (stat/iqr data)))\n    (if (= iqr 0) (map (lambda (x) 0) data)\n        (map (lambda (x) (/ (- x med) iqr)) data))))\n(modified-z-score '(10 12 11 15 14 13 16 17 18 100))"
    :cs-composition "(define (iqr-control-chart data window)\n  ;; Sliding-window IQR to detect variability shifts in time-series data.\n  (let loop ((rest data) (out '()))\n    (if (< (length rest) window) (reverse out)\n        (loop (cdr rest)\n              (cons (stat/iqr (list-head rest window)) out)))))\n(iqr-control-chart '(1 2 2 3 3 3 4 5 10 12 11 14) 5)"
    :emergent "A Tukey outlier detector — an anomaly-flagging routine that emerges from chaining stat/iqr with stat/quartile and a filter; the same shape a box-plot outlier detection or a data-cleaning pipeline uses."
    :meta "This is the fence-based-outlier-detection family — like Tukey's boxplot fences; book-of-composition ch on 'threshold-from-primitives' and SRE Manual ch on outlier flagging.")
   (:eng "The detector is the standard anomaly gate for data-quality pipelines." :math "Fence: (Q1 - 1.5·IQR, Q3 + 1.5·IQR); outliers beyond." :cs "O(n log n) for quartiles + O(n) for filter.")))

(verb
  :name "stat/max" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/max data) -> number | 'nan"
  :summary "Return the largest value in a dataset."
  :explanation "The maximum is the simplest order statistic: the single largest value. Use stat/max when you need to know the upper boundary of your data or to compute range (max − min). It scans the entire list once, keeping track of the running maximum. Empty input returns 'nan (undefined)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/max (list 3 1 4 1 5 9 2 6))" :note "Returns 9.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(stat/max '(2 4 4 6 8))" :note "Returns 8. The maximum of the five values — used as the divisor in normalization.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((data (list 10 12 11 15 14 13 16 17 18 19)))\n  (list 'max (stat/max data) 'min (stat/min data) 'range (- (stat/max data) (stat/min data))))" :note "Combines max and min to compute and display the range together.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (normalize-to-max data)\n  (let ((m (stat/max data)))\n    (if (= m 0) data\n      (map (lambda (x) (/ x m)) data))))\n(normalize-to-max (list 10 20 30 40))" :note "Divides each value by the maximum to scale to [0, 1]; a common preprocessing step.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (running-max series)\n  ;; Compute the running maximum at each position — for tracking all-time-highs.\n  (let loop ((rest (cdr series)) (peak (car series)) (acc (list (car series))))\n    (if (null? rest) (reverse acc)\n        (let ((new-peak (if (> (car rest) peak) (car rest) peak)))\n          (loop (cdr rest) new-peak (cons new-peak acc))))))\n(running-max '(5 3 8 2 10 7 9))" :note "Returns (5 5 8 8 10 10 10) — the running all-time high at each position in the series.")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty list → 'nan; always check before use." "Non-numeric values RAISE — `stat/max: expected a list of numbers, got the string \"a\" in the list` — rather than being compared; ensure data is purely numeric." "Integer overflow is not a concern in Motoi Scheme (unbounded arithmetic), but very large lists incur iteration cost.")
  :drawbacks ("Does not tell you the position or count of the maximum; use core procedures if needed." "Sensitive to outliers; for robust extremes, use stat/quartile or stat/iqr." "O(n) scan time; avoid calling repeatedly on the same data—store the result.")
  :usecases ("Normalize or rescale data to a known upper bound." "Detect data validity: max should fall within expected range." "Compute range as stat/max − stat/min." "Monitor system limits: ensure sensor readings never exceed device maximum.")
  :related ("stat/min" "stat/range" "stat/quartile" "stat/median" "stat/mean")
  :learn (:concept "Order statistics: values selected by their rank (max is rank n, min is rank 1)." :prerequisites ("core: list, map" "stat/min") :progression "Learn max and min as the simplest order statistics; then explore stat/median and stat/quartile for middle ranks.")
  :row-2-audit (
