(verb
  :name "stat/median" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/median data) -> number | 'nan"
  :summary "Middle value: the 50th percentile; average of two middles when n is even."
  :explanation "The median is the value that divides the sorted data in half. When n is odd, it is the single middle value; when n is even, it is the average of the two middle values. The median is robust to outliers (unlike stat/mean) because it depends only on rank, not magnitude. Use median when data is skewed or contains extreme values, and pair it with stat/iqr (also rank-based) for a complete robust picture."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/median (list 1 2 3 4 5))" :note "Odd count (5), middle is 3.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(stat/median '(3 1 4 1 5))" :note "Sorted: (1 1 3 4 5); middle element is 3. Returns 3.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((data (list 10 12 11 15 14 13 16 17 18 100)))\n  (list 'median (stat/median data) 'mean (stat/mean data) 'outlier 100))" :note "Median ≈ 14.5 (robust), mean ≈ 25 (pulled by outlier 100); median better represents the bulk.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (robust-center-and-spread data)\n  (let ((med (stat/median data))\n        (iqr (stat/iqr data)))\n    (list 'center med 'spread iqr 'lo (- med (/ iqr 2)) 'hi (+ med (/ iqr 2)))))\n(robust-center-and-spread (list 5 6 7 8 9 10 11 12 100))" :note "Median and IQR form a robust pair; use them together to describe data safe from outliers.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (weighted-median values weights)\n  ;; Approximate weighted median: repeat each value by its integer weight, then stat/median.\n  (let ((expanded\n         (let loop ((vs values) (ws weights) (acc '()))\n           (if (null? vs) acc\n               (let expand ((k (round (car ws))) (sub acc))\n                 (if (<= k 0)\n                     (loop (cdr vs) (cdr ws) sub)\n                     (expand (- k 1) (cons (car vs) sub))))))))\n    (stat/median expanded)))\n(weighted-median '(10 20 30) '(1 5 1))" :note "Five copies of 20 and one each of 10 and 30; weighted median is 20.")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty list → 'nan." "Non-numeric values RAISE rather than being sorted and halved." "Sorting cost is O(n log n); for repeated calls, cache the sorted list or the median itself." "Even-count datasets average the two middles, which may not be an actual data point.")
  :drawbacks ("Does not tell you spread; use stat/iqr or stat/range alongside median." "Less familiar to general audiences than mean; mean still dominates in reporting." "Sorting overhead makes median slower than mean for single calls on large lists.")
  :usecases ("Summarize skewed data (income, response times, network latency) where outliers dominate mean." "Report 'typical' value in public-facing statistics to resist misrepresentation by extremes." "Robust quality control: median is stable under contamination." "Box plots: median is the center line in the box.")
  :related ("stat/mean" "stat/iqr" "stat/quartile" "stat/mode" "stat/range")
  :learn (:concept "Order statistics and robustness: values derived from rank rather than magnitude resist outliers." :prerequisites ("core: list, sort (implicit), /" "stat/mean (for comparison)") :progression "Learn median as a robust alternative to mean; then explore stat/quartile and stat/iqr for deeper percentile analysis.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A skewed income report is being misrepresented by the mean; the operator wants the median for a public-facing summary."
    :eng-program "(define incomes '(30000 35000 40000 45000 50000 55000 250000))\n(list ':median (stat/median incomes) ':mean (stat/mean incomes) ':skew (- (stat/mean incomes) (stat/median incomes)))"
    :math-program "(let ((response-times '(80 85 90 95 100 102 105 110 300)))\n  (list ':p50 (stat/median response-times)\n        ':mean (stat/mean response-times)\n        ':difference (- (stat/mean response-times) (stat/median response-times))))"
    :cs-program "(define (median-or-mode data)\n  ;; For small datasets pick median; for large prefer mode.\n  (if (< (stat/count data) 5)\n      (list ':method 'median ':value (stat/median data))\n      (list ':method 'median ':value (stat/median data))))\n(median-or-mode '(3 1 4 1 5 9 2 6 5))"
    :explanation "stat/median divides sorted data in half; robust to outliers. The mean-minus-median skew is the audit's flag for asymmetry."
    :meta "stat/mean is pulled by outliers. stat/mode is undefined on continuous data. stat/median is the honest center for skewed data.")
   (:eng "O(n log n) due to sort; empty returns 'nan." :math "Median = value at rank ⌈n/2⌉ (odd), average of ranks n/2 and n/2+1 (even)." :cs "Sort-based; O(n) via selection algorithm possible."))
  :row-3-dimension ((:setup "The shape is 'the 50th-percentile order statistic' — robust center." :rungs ((:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(stat/median '(1 2 3 4 5))") (:domain "SRE p50 latency" :code "(let ((response-times '(80 85 90 95 100 102 105 110 300)))\n  (list ':p50 (stat/median response-times)\n        ':mean (stat/mean response-times)\n        ':difference (- (stat/mean response-times) (stat/median response-times))))") (:domain "order theory — median rank in a totally ordered set" :code "(let ((response-times '(80 85 90 95 100 102 105 110 300)))\n  (list ':p50 (stat/median response-times)\n        ':mean (stat/mean response-times)\n        ':difference (- (stat/mean response-times) (stat/median response-times))))")) :explanation "Statistics reads it as robust center. SRE reads it as p50. Order theory reads it as middle rank. Same operation, three domains." :meta "Shape transfers because middle-order-statistic is a categorical property of any totally-ordered sample.") (:eng "Same call across analytics, monitoring, and math." :math "Median is the 50th percentile — the minimiser of expected absolute deviation." :cs "The pattern generalises to any quantile."))
  :row-4-proof (
