(verb
  :name "stat/sd" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/sd dataset) -> number | 'nan"
  :summary "Population standard deviation: sqrt(sum((x-mu)^2)/n)."
  :impl-status "wired"
  :impl-file "src/wired-verbs.js"
  :impl-notes "Two-pass computation: mean, then sqrt of mean of squared deviations. Empty list returns 0 (NOT 'nan). Single-point returns 0. Uses POPULATION divisor n; for sample SD use stat/sd-sample (divisor n-1)."
  :explanation "SD measures how far data points typically deviate from the mean. Same units as the raw data. Population form (n divisor). Use stat/sd-sample for sample SD (n-1 divisor)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/sd '(2 4 6 8 10))" :note "Returns ~2.83. Mean is 6, so deviations squared and averaged, root-taken.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(stat/sd '(5 5 5 5 5))" :note "All identical values. Returns 0 — zero spread, degenerate case.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((factory-a '(99 100 101 102 100))\n      (factory-b '(95 100 110 105 90)))\n  (list ':a (stat/sd factory-a) ':b (stat/sd factory-b)))" :note "Factory A tighter (SD ~1.02) than Factory B (~7.07). SD as a consistency metric.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (z-of data x)\n  (let ((mu (stat/mean data)) (sig (stat/sd data)))\n    (if (= sig 0) 0 (/ (- x mu) sig))))\n(let ((measurements '(10.1 10.2 10.0 9.9 10.3 10.1 10.2)))\n  (list ':z-10.0 (z-of measurements 10.0)\n        ':z-10.3 (z-of measurements 10.3)))" :note "z-score helper using stat/sd for the denominator. Returns z-values indicating how many SDs each point is from the mean.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (spread-comparison groups)\n  ;; Given a list of (name data) pairs, return one (name sd) record per group,\n  ;; sorted from tightest to widest spread.\n  (let ((pairs (map (lambda (g) (list (car g) (stat/sd (cadr g)))) groups)))\n    (sort pairs (lambda (a b) (< (cadr a) (cadr b))))))\n(spread-comparison\n  (list (list 'a '(99 100 101 102 100))\n        (list 'b '(95 100 110 105 90))\n        (list 'c '(100 100 100 100 100))))" :note "Rank groups by consistency. Returns ((c 0) (a 1.02) (b 7.07)) — group c is perfectly consistent, then a, then b.")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty dataset returns 'nan; guard with stat/count." "This is POPULATION standard deviation (divides by n); use stat/sd-sample for samples." "Single-point dataset returns 0 (zero spread)." "Sensitive to outliers; an extreme value inflates SD more than it does range.")
  :drawbacks ("Assumes continuous or interval data; misleading for categorical or ordinal data." "Interpretation depends on the distribution shape; for non-normal data, stat/iqr is more robust." "Computationally more expensive than stat/range; use range for quick checks." "Often misinterpreted: most data do NOT lie within 1·SD (that depends on distribution shape).")
  :usecases ("Measuring precision or consistency in manufacturing or measurements." "Standardizing data for comparison across different scales (z-scores)." "Hypothesis testing and confidence intervals (assumes normality)." "Quality control: flagging processes with excessive variation.")
  :related ("stat/sd-sample" "stat/variance" "stat/mean" "stat/z-score" "stat/iqr")
  :learn (:concept "Variance and standard deviation: quantifying spread in terms of deviation from the mean" :prerequisites ("stat/mean" "stat/variance") :progression "After stat/sd, learn stat/sd-sample for real-world data, then stat/z-score for standardized comparison, then apply to hypothesis testing with stat/probability.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "Two factories: A produces (99 100 101 102 100), B produces (95 100 110 105 90); the operator wants a consistency metric in the ORIGINAL units so a line manager can read it directly."
    :eng-program "(define fa '(99 100 101 102 100))\n(define fb '(95 100 110 105 90))\n(list ':a-sd (stat/sd fa) ':b-sd (stat/sd fb) ':tighter (if (< (stat/sd fa) (stat/sd fb)) 'a 'b))"
    :math-program "(define (rms-deviation xs)\n  ;; σ = √((1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)²) — root-mean-square deviation about the mean.\n  (stat/sd xs))\n(list ':sigma (rms-deviation '(2 4 6 8 10)) ':flat (rms-deviation '(5 5 5 5 5)))"
    :cs-program "(define (consistency-guard xs)\n  ;; Empty returns 0, so report 'no-data rather than a misleading zero spread.\n  (if (= (stat/count xs) 0) 'no-data (stat/sd xs)))\n(list (consistency-guard '()) (consistency-guard '(99 100 101 102 100)))"
    :explanation "stat/sd gives spread in the same units as the data, so factory A (σ≈1.02) and factory B (σ≈7.07) compare directly — no squared-unit translation needed."
    :meta "stat/variance is in squared units, harder to explain to line managers. stat/iqr is robust but not the classical consistency yardstick. stat/sd is the population RMS-deviation the operator wants for same-unit comparison.")
   (:eng "Population divisor n; empty returns 0 not 'nan." :math "σ = √((1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)²)." :cs "Two-pass; O(n)."))
  :row-3-dimension ((:setup "The shape is 'root-mean-square deviation from the center' — standard deviation." :rungs ((:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(stat/sd '(2 4 6 8 10))") (:domain "signal processing — RMS of a zero-mean signal" :code "(define (rms-deviation xs)\n  ;; σ = √((1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)²) — root-mean-square deviation about the mean.\n  (stat/sd xs))\n(list ':sigma (rms-deviation '(2 4 6 8 10)) ':flat (rms-deviation '(5 5 5 5 5)))") (:domain "L² norm of centred data" :code "(define (rms-deviation xs)\n  ;; σ = √((1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)²) — root-mean-square deviation about the mean.\n  (stat/sd xs))\n(list ':sigma (rms-deviation '(2 4 6 8 10)) ':flat (rms-deviation '(5 5 5 5 5)))")) :explanation "Statistics reads it as spread. DSP reads it as RMS. Functional analysis reads it as L²-norm-of-centred. Same computation, three domains." :meta "Shape transfers because L² norm on centred data is universal.") (:eng "Same call across analytics, DSP, and math." :math "σ = ||X - μ1||_2 / √n." :cs "The pattern generalises to weighted RMS."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "stat/sd is non-negative for every dataset — it is a norm."
    :eng-program "(let ((cases '((2 4 6 8 10) (5 5 5 5) (-3 -1 1 3))))\n  (map (lambda (d) (>= (stat/sd d) 0)) cases))"
    :math-program "(define (sd-nonneg? d) (>= (stat/sd d) 0))\n(list (sd-nonneg? '(-100 -50 -25)) (sd-nonneg? '(0)) (sd-nonneg? '(1.5 2.5 3.5)))"
    :cs-program "(define (sd-is-sqrt-of-variance? d)\n  ;; SD equals √variance, and variance ≥ 0 forces SD ≥ 0.\n  (< (abs (- (stat/sd d) (sqrt (stat/variance d)))) 1e-10))\n(list (sd-is-sqrt-of-variance? '(2 4 6 8 10)) (sd-is-sqrt-of-variance? '(-3 -1 1 3)))"
    :invariant "∀ data. stat/sd(data) ≥ 0 ∧ stat/sd(data) = √(stat/variance(data))"
    :meta "Non-negativity pattern: SD is a norm, ≥ 0, and is the square root of population variance. Book-of-math ch on norms. Falsified by any negative result or sd≠√var.")
   (:eng "Non-negativity is the first axiom of any norm; a cheap sanity check." :math "SD is a square root of a non-negative quantity, so it is real and ≥ 0." :cs "Runnable; all cases return #t."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "stat/sd returns population σ."
    :eng-composition "(define (standardize data)\n  ;; Chain stat/mean and stat/sd to z-normalise data to mean 0, sd 1.\n  (let ((mu (stat/mean data)) (sigma (stat/sd data)))\n    (map (lambda (x) (if (= sigma 0) 0 (/ (- x mu) sigma))) data)))\n(standardize '(10 20 30 40))"
    :math-composition "(define (coefficient-of-variation data)\n  ;; CV = σ/μ — a unit-free relative-spread ratio built from stat/sd and stat/mean.\n  (let ((mu (stat/mean data)) (sigma (stat/sd data)))\n    (if (= mu 0) 'undefined (/ sigma mu))))\n(list ':a (coefficient-of-variation '(99 100 101 102 100))\n      ':b (coefficient-of-variation '(95 100 110 105 90)))"
    :cs-composition "(define (control-limits data k)\n  ;; μ ± k·σ — the classic Shewhart control-chart band from stat/mean and stat/sd.\n  (let ((mu (stat/mean data)) (sigma (stat/sd data)))\n    (list ':lower (- mu (* k sigma)) ':center mu ':upper (+ mu (* k sigma)))))\n(control-limits '(99 100 101 102 100) 3)"
    :emergent "Z-normalisation and its cousins (CV, control bands) — a centre-and-scale family that emerges from chaining stat/mean with stat/sd; the same shape a StandardScaler, a coefficient-of-variation report, or a Shewhart control chart uses."
    :meta "This is the standardisation family — like sklearn's StandardScaler, batch-norm's per-batch normalisation, or SPC control limits; book-of-composition ch on 'centre-and-scale' and book-of-ml ch on feature standardisation.")
   (:eng "The transform is the default preprocessing for many ML pipelines and the SPC control chart the operator watches." :math "z = (x-μ)/σ; CV = σ/μ; band = μ ± kσ." :cs "O(n); a pure map after two O(n) reductions.")))

(verb
  :name "stat/sd-sample" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/sd-sample dataset) -> number | 'nan | (escalate no-spread)"
  :summary "Sample standard deviation using Bessel's n-1 correction."
  :impl-status "wired"
  :impl-file "src/wired-verbs.js"
  :impl-notes "Sample SD: mean, then sqrt of sum-of-squared-deviations/(n-1). Empty or single-element list returns 0 (NOT 'no-spread or 'nan as older docs claimed). Impl is permissive on degenerate cases."
  :explanation "Sample SD divides by n-1 instead of n, correcting for the bias from estimating the mean from the same sample. Unbiased estimator of population SD for the underlying distribution."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/sd-sample '(5 10 15 20 25))" :note "Returns ~7.91. Divides by n-1=4 instead of n=5. Compare with stat/sd for the same data.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(stat/sd-sample '(5))" :note "Only one data point. Returns 0 (not enough spread info); the impl doesn't raise. Guard with (>= (stat/count data) 2) if the caller needs a signaled underspec.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((sample '(100 105 110 95 120)))\n  (list ':sample-sd (stat/sd-sample sample)\n        ':pop-sd    (stat/sd sample)))" :note "Sample SD (~9.62) is larger than population SD (~8.60) — Bessel's correction inflates by sqrt(n/(n-1)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (confidence-interval trials z)\n  (let* ((n (stat/count trials))\n         (mu (stat/mean trials))\n         (se (/ (stat/sd-sample trials) (sqrt n))))\n    (list ':mean mu\n          ':se se\n          ':lower (- mu (* z se))\n          ':upper (+ mu (* z se)))))\n(confidence-interval '(2.5 2.6 2.4 2.7 2.5 2.6) 1.96)" :note "Approx 95% CI via z=1.96. Sample SD feeds standard error which feeds the interval width.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (t-statistic sample hyp-mean)\n  ;; Compute a one-sample t-statistic against a hypothesized mean.\n  (let* ((n (stat/count sample))\n         (mu (stat/mean sample))\n         (sd (stat/sd-sample sample)))\n    (if (or (< n 2) (= sd 0)) 'no-t\n        (/ (- mu hyp-mean) (/ sd (sqrt n))))))\n(list (t-statistic '(100 102 101 99 100) 100)\n      (t-statistic '(5) 3))" :note "Text-book t-stat helper. Returns (~0.78 no-t) — the first sample's t-stat is small (mean close to hypothesis relative to sampling variability); the second is too small to compute.")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty dataset returns 'nan; guard with stat/count." "Dataset with fewer than 2 points escalates 'no-spread; cannot estimate spread from 0 or 1 observation." "Assumes data are a random sample; if data are already a population, use stat/sd instead." "Larger than stat/sd for the same data due to Bessel's correction (n−1 vs n).")
  :drawbacks ("Bessel's correction introduces a slight bias toward the high side for small samples; there is no perfect unbiased estimator of SD." "Sensitive to outliers; a single extreme value inflates the sample SD." "Assumes continuous or interval data; misleading for categorical or ordinal data." "Computationally more expensive than stat/range; use range for quick checks.")
  :usecases ("Computing standard error for confidence intervals from sample data." "Hypothesis testing and t-tests (where sample SD is the natural choice)." "Reporting variability in experimental or survey data." "Estimating population SD when you have only a sample.")
  :related ("stat/sd" "stat/variance-sample" "stat/mean" "stat/z-score" "stat/iqr")
  :learn (:concept "Bessel's correction: why sample statistics divide by n−1 to correct for bias" :prerequisites ("stat/sd" "stat/variance" "stat/mean") :progression "After stat/sd-sample, learn stat/variance-sample for the squared form, then apply to confidence intervals and hypothesis testing.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "An A/B test needs a 95% CI on a mean from n=6 samples; the operator must use sample SD (n-1) for the unbiased estimator of the population, not the population SD (n)."
    :eng-program "(define trials '(2.5 2.6 2.4 2.7 2.5 2.6))\n(let* ((n (stat/count trials)) (mu (stat/mean trials))\n       (se (/ (stat/sd-sample trials) (sqrt n))))\n  (list ':mean mu ':lower (- mu (* 1.96 se)) ':upper (+ mu (* 1.96 se))))"
    :math-program "(define (bessel-inflation xs)\n  ;; s / σ should equal √(n/(n-1)) — the correction factor.\n  (let ((n (stat/count xs)))\n    (list ':s (stat/sd-sample xs) ':sigma (stat/sd xs)\n          ':factor (sqrt (/ n (- n 1))))))\n(bessel-inflation '(100 105 110 95 120))"
    :cs-program "(define (sample-sd-guard xs)\n  ;; Below 2 points there is no spread to estimate; report explicitly.\n  (if (< (stat/count xs) 2) 'too-small (stat/sd-sample xs)))\n(list (sample-sd-guard '(5)) (sample-sd-guard '(5 10 15 20 25)))"
    :explanation "stat/sd-sample uses n-1 to give an unbiased estimator; the operator feeds it into the standard-error formula se = s/√n and builds the interval around the mean."
    :meta "stat/sd (population) underestimates the population SD when computed from a small sample and would narrow the CI dishonestly. stat/sd-sample is the inferential default.")
   (:eng "Empty or single-point returns 0; n-1 divisor." :math "s = √((1/(n-1)) Σ (xᵢ-x̄)²)." :cs "Two-pass; O(n)."))
  :row-3-dimension ((:setup "The shape is 'unbiased estimator of population SD from a sample'." :rungs ((:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(stat/sd-sample '(5 10 15 20 25))") (:domain "clinical trial arms — sample SD of a treatment group" :code "(define (bessel-inflation xs)\n  ;; s / σ should equal √(n/(n-1)) — the correction factor.\n  (let ((n (stat/count xs)))\n    (list ':s (stat/sd-sample xs) ':sigma (stat/sd xs)\n          ':factor (sqrt (/ n (- n 1))))))\n(bessel-inflation '(100 105 110 95 120))") (:domain "Bayesian conjugate updates — sample-scale parameter in a normal-gamma model" :code "(define (bessel-inflation xs)\n  ;; s / σ should equal √(n/(n-1)) — the correction factor.\n  (let ((n (stat/count xs)))\n    (list ':s (stat/sd-sample xs) ':sigma (stat/sd xs)\n          ':factor (sqrt (/ n (- n 1))))))\n(bessel-inflation '(100 105 110 95 120))")) :explanation "Statistics reads it as sample-SD. Clinical trials read it as arm-variability. Bayesian reads it as scale hyperparameter update. Same operation, three domains." :meta "Shape transfers because unbiased estimation is a categorical property of many inference frameworks.") (:eng "Same call across A/B testing, biostats, and Bayesian inference." :math "Bessel-corrected SD is E[s²] = σ² unbiased." :cs "The pattern generalises to any Bessel-corrected estimator."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "stat/sd-sample equals stat/sd multiplied by √(n/(n-1)) for n ≥ 2 — the Bessel identity."
    :eng-program "(let ((d '(2 4 6 8 10)))\n  (< (abs (- (stat/sd-sample d) (* (stat/sd d) (sqrt (/ (length d) (- (length d) 1)))))) 1e-10))"
    :math-program "(define (bessel-identity? d)\n  (let ((n (length d)))\n    (< (abs (- (stat/sd-sample d) (* (stat/sd d) (sqrt (/ n (- n 1)))))) 1e-10)))\n(list (bessel-identity? '(1 3 5 7 9)) (bessel-identity? '(100 105 110 95 120)))"
    :cs-program "(define (sample-ge-population? d)\n  ;; Bessel's correction always inflates: s ≥ σ for n ≥ 2.\n  (>= (stat/sd-sample d) (stat/sd d)))\n(list (sample-ge-population? '(2 4 6 8 10)) (sample-ge-population? '(72 75 73 76 74 71)))"
    :invariant "∀ data with n≥2. stat/sd-sample(data) = stat/sd(data)·√(n/(n-1)) ∧ stat/sd-sample(data) ≥ stat/sd(data)"
    :meta "Bessel-correction identity pattern: the sample SD is a fixed positive rescaling of the population SD, always ≥ it. Book-of-math ch on unbiased estimators. Falsified by any deviation.")
   (:eng "Identity confirms the Bessel scaling; the ≥ relation is a quick monotone sanity check." :math "s = σ·√(n/(n-1)) by definition, and √(n/(n-1)) > 1." :cs "Runnable; tolerance-based equality plus a boolean."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "stat/sd-sample returns Bessel-corrected SD."
    :eng-composition "(define (welch-t sample-a sample-b)\n  ;; Chain stat/sd-sample with stat/mean to build a Welch two-sample t.\n  (let* ((na (stat/count sample-a)) (nb (stat/count sample-b))\n         (ma (stat/mean sample-a)) (mb (stat/mean sample-b))\n         (va (expt (stat/sd-sample sample-a) 2))\n         (vb (expt (stat/sd-sample sample-b) 2)))\n    (/ (- ma mb) (sqrt (+ (/ va na) (/ vb nb))))))\n(welch-t '(10 12 14 16 18) '(15 17 19 21 23))"
    :math-composition "(define (standard-error sample)\n  ;; SE = s/√n — the sampling-distribution spread of the mean estimator.\n  (let ((n (stat/count sample)))\n    (if (< n 2) 'undefined (/ (stat/sd-sample sample) (sqrt n)))))\n(list ':se (standard-error '(2.5 2.6 2.4 2.7 2.5 2.6)))"
    :cs-composition "(define (cohens-d a b)\n  ;; Effect size = (mean-diff) / pooled-sd, a scale-free comparison of two samples.\n  (let* ((na (stat/count a)) (nb (stat/count b))\n         (sa (stat/sd-sample a)) (sb (stat/sd-sample b))\n         (pooled (sqrt (/ (+ (* (- na 1) (* sa sa)) (* (- nb 1) (* sb sb)))\n                          (- (+ na nb) 2)))))\n    (if (= pooled 0) 0 (/ (- (stat/mean a) (stat/mean b)) pooled))))\n(cohens-d '(10 12 14 16 18) '(15 17 19 21 23))"
    :emergent "A family of inferential statistics — Welch's t, the standard error of the mean, and Cohen's d effect size — that emerges from chaining stat/sd-sample with stat/mean and stat/count; the same shapes scipy.stats.ttest_ind or an effect-size report use."
    :meta "This is the two-sample-inferential family — like Welch t, Student t, standard error, and Cohen's d; book-of-composition ch on 'test-statistic-from-primitives' and book-of-ml ch on hypothesis testing.")
   (:eng "The t-stat, SE, and effect size are the standard hypothesis-test primitives; downstream code produces p-values and confidence intervals." :math "t = (x̄_A - x̄_B)/√(s_A²/n_A + s_B²/n_B); SE = s/√n; d = (x̄_A-x̄_B)/s_pooled." :cs "O(n_A + n_B); scalar test statistics.")))

(verb
  :name "stat/sum" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/sum dataset) -> number"
  :summary "Calculate the sum of all values in a dataset: Σx."
  :explanation "Sum is the foundation for many other statistics: the mean divides the sum by the count, variance squares deviations from the mean, etc. Sum is useful on its own for totaling quantities (e.g., total revenue, total distance). An empty dataset returns 0 (the additive identity). A non-numeric element raises rather than being folded in."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/sum (list 10 20 30 40))" :note "Returns 100; the total of all four values")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((daily-sales (list 150 200 180 220 190)))\n  (let ((weekly-total (stat/sum daily-sales))\n        (daily-avg (/ (stat/sum daily-sales) (stat/count daily-sales))))\n    (list 'weekly-total weekly-total 'daily-average daily-avg)))" :note "Sums daily sales and divides by count to compute average")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((transactions (list -50 100 -25 200 75)))\n  (let ((net (stat/sum transactions)))\n    (if (>= net 0)\n      (display (string-append \"Net gain: \" (number->string net)))\n      (display (string-append \"Net loss: \" (number->string (abs net)))))))" :note "Sums signed values (gains and losses); the result is the net change")
  )
