(verb
  :name "stat/variance" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/variance dataset) -> number | 'nan"
  :summary "Calculate the population variance: Σ(x−μ)²/n, the squared deviation from the mean."
  :explanation "Variance measures how spread out data are by averaging the squared deviations from the mean. Squaring makes all deviations positive and emphasizes large deviations. The result is in squared units of the original data (e.g., dollars² if data are dollars), which is why standard deviation (the square root) is often more intuitive. A single-point dataset returns 0 (zero spread); an empty dataset returns 'nan."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/variance (list 1 3 5 7 9))" :note "Returns 8; measure of squared deviation from the mean (5)")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((group-a (list 50 50 50))\n      (group-b (list 40 50 60)))\n  (list 'group-a-variance (stat/variance group-a)\n        'group-b-variance (stat/variance group-b)))" :note "Group A (variance 0) is perfectly uniform; Group B (variance ≈ 67) is more spread out despite having the same mean")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((before-intervention (list 85 88 90 87 89 91)))\n  (let ((var-before (stat/variance before-intervention))\n        (sd-before (stat/sd before-intervention)))\n    (display (string-append \"Variance: \" (number->string var-before) \", \"\n                            \"StdDev: \" (number->string sd-before)))))" :note "Computes both variance and its square root (SD) to understand spread in original units")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty dataset returns 'nan; guard with stat/count." "Variance is in squared units; interpret with care (e.g., dollars² is hard to visualize)." "Single-point dataset returns 0." "This is POPULATION variance (divides by n); use stat/variance-sample for samples (divides by n−1)." "Sensitive to outliers; squared deviations amplify the effect of extreme values.")
  :drawbacks ("Squared units make variance hard to interpret directly; use stat/sd instead." "Variance is mathematically convenient but not intuitive for non-statisticians." "For non-normal distributions, stat/iqr or stat/range may be more robust." "Computationally more expensive than stat/range; use range for initial checks.")
  :usecases ("Theoretical statistics and hypothesis testing (variance is the basis for many tests)." "Computing standard deviation (SD = √variance)." "Comparing variability across groups or time periods." "Quantifying precision in measurement systems (lower variance = higher precision).")
  :related ("stat/variance-sample" "stat/sd" "stat/mean" "stat/range" "stat/iqr")
  :learn (:concept "Variance: measuring spread as average squared deviation from the mean" :prerequisites ("stat/mean" "stat/sum") :progression "After variance, learn stat/sd (square root of variance for intuitive units), then stat/variance-sample (Bessel's correction), then apply to hypothesis testing.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A precision analysis needs the variance in SQUARED units for uncertainty propagation; the operator's downstream math (error bars for a sum of independent measurements) adds σ² values, not σ values."
    :eng-program "(define err-a '(0.1 -0.2 0.1 0.0 -0.1))\n(define err-b '(0.3 -0.1 0.2 -0.3 0.0))\n(list ':var-a (stat/variance err-a) ':var-b (stat/variance err-b)\n      ':var-sum (+ (stat/variance err-a) (stat/variance err-b)))"
    :math-program "(define (second-central-moment xs)\n  ;; σ² = (1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)² — the mean squared deviation about μ.\n  (stat/variance xs))\n(list ':spread (second-central-moment '(1 3 5 7 9))\n      ':flat (second-central-moment '(5 5 5)))"
    :cs-program "(define (variance-vs-sd data)\n  ;; Variance is in squared units; SD (its root) is in original units.\n  (list ':variance (stat/variance data)\n        ':sd (stat/sd data)\n        ':sd-is-root? (< (abs (- (stat/sd data) (sqrt (stat/variance data)))) 1e-10)))\n(variance-vs-sd '(1 3 5 7 9))"
    :explanation "stat/variance returns σ² directly; the propagation formula Var(A+B) = Var(A) + Var(B) for independent errors sums variances, so squared units are exactly what the downstream math wants."
    :meta "stat/sd returns σ (would need squaring). stat/sd-sample uses Bessel's n-1. stat/variance is the population-form second moment in squared units — the right primitive when the formula uses σ² directly.")
   (:eng "Population divisor n; empty returns 'nan." :math "σ² = (1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)²." :cs "Two-pass; O(n)."))
  :row-3-dimension ((:setup "The shape is 'average squared deviation from the mean' — variance." :rungs ((:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(stat/variance '(1 3 5 7 9))") (:domain "error propagation — variance-of-sum = sum-of-variances for independent errors" :code "(define (second-central-moment xs)\n  ;; σ² = (1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)² — the mean squared deviation about μ.\n  (stat/variance xs))\n(list ':spread (second-central-moment '(1 3 5 7 9))\n      ':flat (second-central-moment '(5 5 5)))") (:domain "probability theory — Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])²" :code "(define (second-central-moment xs)\n  ;; σ² = (1/n) Σ (xᵢ-μ)² — the mean squared deviation about μ.\n  (stat/variance xs))\n(list ':spread (second-central-moment '(1 3 5 7 9))\n      ':flat (second-central-moment '(5 5 5)))")) :explanation "Statistics reads it as spread. Physics reads it as noise power. Probability reads it as second central moment. Same computation, three domains." :meta "Shape transfers because the second central moment is a categorical property.") (:eng "Same call across analytics, physics, and math." :math "Second central moment E[(X-μ)²]." :cs "The pattern is the base case for higher moments."))
  :row-4-proof (
