    :emergent "A family of two-sample inferential quantities — Welch's degrees of freedom, the variance-ratio F statistic, and the pooled sample variance — that emerges from chaining stat/variance-sample with stat/count; the same shapes an unequal-variance t-test, an F-test, or a pooled two-sample t use."
    :meta "This is the DoF-and-variance-test family — like Welch-Satterthwaite, the F-test for equal variances, and pooled-variance t; book-of-composition ch on 'approximate-df-formulas' and book-of-ml ch on t-tests.")
   (:eng "Welch DoF, the F ratio, and the pooled variance are the standard building blocks for two-sample tests." :math "DoF = (v_a/n_a + v_b/n_b)²/((v_a/n_a)²/(n_a-1)+(v_b/n_b)²/(n_b-1)); F = s_a²/s_b²; s_p² = ((n_a-1)s_a²+(n_b-1)s_b²)/(n_a+n_b-2)." :cs "O(n_a + n_b); scalars.")))

(verb
  :name "stat/z-score" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stat/z-score dataset value) -> number | 'nan | (escalate no-spread)"
  :summary "Calculate the population z-score: (value−μ)/σ, measuring how many standard deviations a value is from the mean."
  :explanation "A z-score standardizes a value by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation, placing it on a unit-free scale where 0 means the value equals the mean. Positive z-scores are above the mean; negative are below. Z-scores enable comparison across different datasets, different units, or different distributions. If the standard deviation is zero (all data are identical), the verb escalates 'no-spread because division by zero is undefined. An empty dataset returns 'nan."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(stat/z-score (list 100 110 120 130 140) 120)" :note "Returns 0; the value 120 equals the mean, so z = 0")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((sat-scores (list 450 500 550 600 650)))\n  (list (stat/z-score sat-scores 550)\n        (stat/z-score sat-scores 650)\n        (stat/z-score sat-scores 350)))" :note "Standardizes three scores: 550 is at the mean (z=0), 650 is 1 SD above (z≈1), 350 is 2 SD below (z≈−2)")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (filter pred xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (acc '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (reverse acc))\n          ((pred (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs) (cons (car xs) acc)))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) acc)))))\n(let ((iq-scores (list 85 90 100 105 110 115 130)))\n  (let ((outlier-threshold 2.5))\n    (let ((flagged (filter (lambda (x)\n                            (> (abs (stat/z-score iq-scores x)) outlier-threshold))\n                          iq-scores)))\n      (display (string-append \"Outliers (|z| > \" (number->string outlier-threshold) \"): \"\n                             (number->string (length flagged)))))))" :note "Flags outliers by finding scores more than 2.5 SDs from the mean")
  )
  :caveats ("Empty dataset returns 'nan; guard with stat/count." "All data points identical (SD = 0) escalates 'no-spread; no z-score can be computed." "Z-scores are meaningful only for continuous or interval data; misleading for ordinal or categorical data." "Uses population SD (divides by n); for sample-based z-scores, use stat/sd-sample in a custom calculation.")
  :drawbacks ("Interpretation assumes normal or approximately normal distribution; for non-normal data, z-scores are less informative." "A z-score of 2 does NOT mean 95% of data fall within ±2 SD unless the distribution is normal (it's a common misunderstanding)." "Z-scores can be misleading if the dataset is small or skewed; inspect the distribution first." "Sensitive to outliers in the computation of mean and SD; extreme values distort the scale.")
  :usecases ("Standardizing test scores across different scales (e.g., SAT vs. ACT)." "Detecting outliers: values with |z| > 3 are rare (assuming normality) and may warrant investigation." "Comparing individual performance to group norms (e.g., 'your score is 1.5 SDs above the mean')." "Preparing data for machine learning: z-score normalization is a common preprocessing step." "Hypothesis testing: z-tests use z-scores to compute p-values.")
  :related ("stat/mean" "stat/sd" "stat/variance" "stat/quartile" "stat/probability")
  :learn (:concept "Standardization: converting raw scores to a unit-free scale for fair comparison" :prerequisites ("stat/mean" "stat/sd") :progression "After z-scores, learn stat/probability for computing cumulative probabilities, then apply to hypothesis testing and confidence intervals.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A grading lane compares a student's SAT (600 in one population) and ACT (28 in another); the operator wants both on the same unit-free z scale so a fair comparison is possible across the two different scoring systems."
    :eng-program "(define sat-population '(450 500 550 600 650))\n(define act-population '(18 22 24 26 30))\n(list ':sat-z (stat/z-score sat-population 600)\n      ':act-z (stat/z-score act-population 28))"
    :math-program "(define (standardize-one dist x)\n  ;; z = (x - μ)/σ — subtract the mean, scale by the population SD.\n  (stat/z-score dist x))\n(list ':at-mean (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) 120)\n      ':one-sd-up (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) (+ 120 (stat/sd '(100 110 120 130 140)))))"
    :cs-program "(define (z-guard dist x)\n  ;; A degenerate distribution (σ=0) has no z-scale; report it rather than dividing by zero.\n  (if (= (stat/sd dist) 0) 'no-spread (stat/z-score dist x)))\n(list (z-guard '(5 5 5 5) 5) (z-guard '(450 500 550 600 650) 600))"
    :explanation "stat/z-score standardises to (value-μ)/σ; the operator compares 600-on-SAT and 28-on-ACT on a unit-free scale where the sign and magnitude mean the same thing in both populations."
    :meta "stat/probability is different (favourable/total). stat/quartile gives a rank not a z. stat/z-score is the standardisation-specific verb using population σ.")
   (:eng "Empty escalates 'nan; σ=0 escalates 'no-spread." :math "z = (x-μ)/σ." :cs "Two aggregations plus O(1) scaling."))
  :row-3-dimension ((:setup "The shape is 'standardise a value to unit-free scale' — normalisation." :rungs ((:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(stat/z-score '(100 110 120) 110)") (:domain "ML preprocessing — per-feature normalisation for gradient descent stability" :code "(define (standardize-one dist x)\n  ;; z = (x - μ)/σ — subtract the mean, scale by the population SD.\n  (stat/z-score dist x))\n(list ':at-mean (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) 120)\n      ':one-sd-up (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) (+ 120 (stat/sd '(100 110 120 130 140)))))") (:domain "hypothesis testing — z-statistic under normality" :code "(define (standardize-one dist x)\n  ;; z = (x - μ)/σ — subtract the mean, scale by the population SD.\n  (stat/z-score dist x))\n(list ':at-mean (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) 120)\n      ':one-sd-up (standardize-one '(100 110 120 130 140) (+ 120 (stat/sd '(100 110 120 130 140)))))")) :explanation "Statistics reads it as z-score. ML preprocessing reads it as feature normalisation. Hypothesis testing reads it as a test statistic. Same operation, three domains." :meta "Shape transfers because standardisation is a universal preprocessing.") (:eng "Same call across analytics, ML, and inference." :math "z is unit-free." :cs "The pattern generalises to any (value-location)/scale transform."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "stat/z-score of the mean is zero, and z scales by 1/k when the whole distribution and value are multiplied by k>0."
    :eng-program "(let ((d '(100 110 120 130 140)))\n  (< (abs (stat/z-score d (stat/mean d))) 1e-10))"
    :math-program "(define (z-at-mean-zero? d)\n  (< (abs (stat/z-score d (stat/mean d))) 1e-10))\n(list (z-at-mean-zero? '(1 2 3 4 5)) (z-at-mean-zero? '(-10 0 10 20 30)))"
    :cs-program "(define (z-scale-invariant? d x k)\n  ;; Scaling data and value by k>0 leaves z unchanged (both μ and σ scale by k).\n  (let ((scaled-d (map (lambda (v) (* k v)) d)))\n    (< (abs (- (stat/z-score scaled-d (* k x)) (stat/z-score d x))) 1e-10)))\n(list (z-scale-invariant? '(100 110 120 130 140) 130 5)\n      (z-scale-invariant? '(2 4 6 8 10) 8 3))"
    :invariant "∀ non-degenerate data. stat/z-score(data, mean(data)) = 0 ∧ ∀ k>0. z(k·data, k·x) = z(data, x)"
    :meta "Origin-and-scale-invariance pattern: μ is the anchor at 0 and z is invariant to a common positive rescaling, because both μ and σ scale by k. Book-of-math ch on z-scores. Falsified by any residual at the mean or any scaling break.")
   (:eng "Anchor invariant confirms the centring; scale-invariance is why z compares across units." :math "z = (μ-μ)/σ = 0; z(kx) = (kx-kμ)/(kσ) = z(x)." :cs "Runnable; both tolerance-based."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "stat/z-score standardises a value against a distribution."
    :eng-composition "(define (anomaly-detector data threshold)\n  ;; Chain stat/z-score to flag values beyond a z threshold.\n  (filter (lambda (x) (> (abs (stat/z-score data x)) threshold)) data))\n(anomaly-detector '(85 90 100 105 110 115 130) 2.0)"
    :math-composition "(define (z-normalise data)\n  ;; Map every point to its z-score — the full standardised distribution.\n  (map (lambda (x) (stat/z-score data x)) data))\n(let ((z (z-normalise '(10 20 30 40 50))))\n  (list ':z z ':mean-of-z (stat/mean z) ':sd-of-z (stat/sd z)))"
    :cs-composition "(define (rank-by-extremity data)\n  ;; Sort points by |z| descending — the most-surprising values first.\n  (sort (map (lambda (x) (list x (abs (stat/z-score data x)))) data)\n        (lambda (a b) (> (cadr a) (cadr b)))))\n(rank-by-extremity '(85 90 100 105 110 115 130))"
    :emergent "A standardisation-and-flagging family — a z-based anomaly gate, a full z-normalised distribution (mean 0, sd 1), and an extremity ranking — that emerges from chaining stat/z-score with a filter, a map, or a sort; the same shapes a Chauvenet's criterion, a StandardScaler, or a control chart use."
    :meta "This is the threshold-anomaly family — like Chauvenet, the three-sigma rule, or SPC control charts; book-of-composition ch on 'threshold-detector' and SRE Manual ch on anomaly rules.")
   (:eng "The detector is the standard normal-based outlier gate; z-normalisation feeds any downstream model." :math "|z| > τ ⇒ outlier; z-normalised data has mean 0 and sd 1." :cs "O(n·n) naive (recomputes μ,σ per call) — precompute μ,σ once for O(n).")))


;; ═══ base-library aggregates and rate measures (bare names) ═══
;; The verbs below live in the base library — they carry no `stat/` prefix —
;; but they are statistics, so they are documented here beside their
;; namespaced siblings. Authored 2026-08-02 (reference-fill, numeric slice).

(verb
  :name "sum" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(sum lst) -> number"
  :summary "Add every element of a list together, starting from 0."
  :explanation "The plainest aggregate there is: fold + over a list with 0 as the seed. Because 0 is the seed, the empty list answers 0 — and that is the right answer, since 0 is the identity of addition and 'nothing added up' really is zero. sum is the base-library twin of stat/sum; the two agree on every list of numbers. Reach for sum when you just want a total, and for stat/sum when you are already working inside the stat family and want the naming to say so. The mean is sum divided by count, which is why almost every statistic in this chapter begins with a sum."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sum (list 1 2 3))" :note "6 — one plus two plus three.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(sum (list))" :note "0 — the empty total is zero, because zero is the identity of addition.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(sum (list -5 5))" :note "0 — the negatives cancel the positives; a zero total does not mean an empty list.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(sum (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 1 2 3 4)))" :note "30 — square first, then total: the sum of squares, the core of variance.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (running-totals xs)\n  (let loop ((rest xs) (seen (list)) (out (list)))\n    (if (null? rest)\n        (reverse out)\n        (let ((seen2 (append seen (list (car rest)))))\n          (loop (cdr rest) seen2 (cons (sum seen2) out))))))\n(display (running-totals (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(newline)" :note "(1 3 6 10) — a prefix-sum series built by summing each growing prefix.")
  )
  :caveats ("Every element must be a number. (sum (list 1 \"a\" 3)) raises `sum: expected a list of numbers, got the string \"a\" in the list`. Until this was fixed on 2026-08-02 the same call answered the STRING \"1a3\", because the fold underneath is the host's +, which becomes concatenation the moment a string appears — a verb contracted to return a number handing back a string with no error." "The argument must be a LIST. (sum 5) raises `sum: expected a list of numbers, got the number 5`, and (sum (vector 1 2 3)) says the same of a vector. A vector is not a list; pass it through vector->list first." "The empty list answers 0, so 0 cannot distinguish 'no data' from 'the data totalled zero'. Check the length first if that distinction matters." "Exact fractions are accepted and totalled as decimals: (sum (list 1/2 1/2)) is 1." "A long sum of decimals carries float error: (sum (list 0.1 0.2)) is 0.30000000000000004.")
  :drawbacks ("No seed argument — you cannot fold from a starting balance; add it yourself afterwards." "No weighted variant; multiply before summing if the elements carry weights." "Linear in the list length with no running-total form, so repeated prefix sums are quadratic.")
  :usecases ("Total a column of numbers before dividing to get a mean." "Accumulate a sum of squares for variance and standard deviation." "Add up money amounts in a small ledger.")
  :related ("stat/sum" "mean" "stat/mean" "stat/count" "+")
  :learn (:concept "Aggregation: collapsing many numbers into one by folding an associative operation with its identity." :prerequisites ("list" "+" "map") :progression "Learn sum, then mean (sum over count), then stat/variance (a sum of squared deviations over count).")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A shop ledger holds one number per sale and the operator wants the day's take as a single figure — not a per-sale report, just the total."
    :eng-program "(define sales (list 12 40 7 33))\n(display (list 'n (length sales) 'total (sum sales)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (sum-identity? xs)\n  (= (sum xs) (+ (sum (list (car xs))) (sum (cdr xs)))))\n(display (sum-identity? (list 4 8 15)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (safe-sum xs)\n  (if (list? xs) (sum xs) 'not-a-list))\n(display (list (safe-sum (list 1 2)) (safe-sum 5)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: pair the total with the count so the reader knows what it was made from. math: splitting the list and re-summing gives the same answer — associativity, witnessed. cs: sum refuses a non-list itself, so the guard here is about choosing your own answer for that case rather than taking the error."
    :meta "Reach for sum and NOT mean: sum answers 'how much altogether', mean answers 'how much each'. A total of 92 across 4 sales is not 92 per sale.")
   (:eng "One pass over the list; the count must be carried separately." :math "Σxᵢ, with the empty sum equal to 0." :cs "A left fold of + over a list with identity 0."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'collapse a sequence with an associative operator and its identity'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (sum (list 3 6 9)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "accounting — a day's takings" :code "(define takings (list 120 45 300))\n(display (list 'gross (sum takings)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "physics — total mass of a parts list" :code "(define masses (list 2.5 0.5 1.0))\n(display (list 'kg (sum masses)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Statistics reads it as a total. Accounting reads it as gross revenue. Physics reads it as total mass. The operation never changes; only the unit attached to the answer does."
    :meta "Every monoid fold has this shape: an associative operator, an identity, and a sequence.")
   (:eng "Same call wherever a list of quantities must become one quantity." :math "A monoid fold over (ℝ, +, 0)." :cs "reduce with an explicit identity — safe on the empty case."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "sum is additive over concatenation: the total of two lists joined equals the sum of their totals."
    :eng-program "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 10 20))\n(display (= (sum (append a b)) (+ (sum a) (sum b))))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (additive? a b)\n  (= (sum (append a b)) (+ (sum a) (sum b))))\n(display (list (additive? (list 1 2) (list 3))\n               (additive? (list) (list 5 5))\n               (additive? (list 7) (list))))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (identity-holds? xs)\n  (= (sum (append (list) xs)) (sum xs)))\n(display (identity-holds? (list 4 4 4)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ lists a b of numbers. sum(append a b) = sum(a) + sum(b), and sum(()) = 0"
    :meta "Monoid-homomorphism pattern: sum maps list-append to number-addition and the empty list to 0. Falsified by any pair of lists whose joined total differs from the two totals added.")
   (:eng "Additivity is what lets you total per-branch and then total the branches." :math "sum is a monoid homomorphism (List ℝ, ++, ()) → (ℝ, +, 0)." :cs "The property that makes a parallel or chunked sum safe."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "sum collapses a list of numbers to their total."
    :eng-composition "(define (share-of-total xs)\n  (let ((t (sum xs)))\n    (map (lambda (x) (pct x t)) xs)))\n(display (share-of-total (list 25 25 50)))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (sum-of-squared-deviations xs)\n  (let ((m (mean xs)))\n    (sum (map (lambda (x) (* (- x m) (- x m))) xs))))\n(display (sum-of-squared-deviations (list 2 4 6)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (chunked-sum chunks)\n  (sum (map sum chunks)))\n(display (chunked-sum (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4) (list 5))))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A composition breakdown emerges: total once, then express every element as its share of that total — the shape behind pie charts, budget splits, and market-share tables."
    :meta "This is the normalise-by-total family — the same move that turns counts into probabilities and votes into percentages.")
   (:eng "Shares always sum back to 100, which is the check that the breakdown is complete." :math "xᵢ/Σx is the discrete probability mass built from counts." :cs "Chunked summing is map-then-reduce; additivity is what makes it correct.")))

(verb
  :name "mean" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(mean lst) -> number | 'nan"
  :summary "The arithmetic average of a list: its total divided by how many numbers it holds."
  :explanation "Add everything up, then divide by the count. The mean is the balance point of the data — the place where the deviations above cancel the deviations below — and it is the first thing anyone asks for when a list of numbers needs to become one number. It is also fragile: a single extreme value drags it, which is why stat/median sits beside it. mean is the base-library twin of stat/mean and agrees with it on every list of numbers. mean averages over machine floats rather than over exact rationals, so the mean of whole numbers is often a decimal: the mean of 1 2 3 4 is 2.5, not the exact 5/2 that (/ (sum (list 1 2 3 4)) 4) answers. Reach for / when you want the fraction."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(mean (list 2 4 6))" :note "4 — twelve shared between three.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(mean (list 1 2 3 4))" :note "2.5 — the average need not be one of the values, and it comes back as a decimal because mean works in floats. (/ (sum (list 1 2 3 4)) 4) is the same number written exactly, 5/2.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(mean (list 10))" :note "10 — one number averages to itself.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(= (mean (list 3 5 7)) (/ (sum (list 3 5 7)) 3))" :note "#t — mean is exactly sum divided by count; nothing is hidden inside it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (mean-is-balance-point? xs)\n  (let ((m (mean xs)))\n    (< (abs (sum (map (lambda (x) (- x m)) xs))) 1e-9)))\n(display (mean-is-balance-point? (list 2 4 4 6 9)))\n(newline)" :note "#t — the signed deviations from the mean always cancel; that is what makes the mean the balance point.")
  )
  :caveats ("The empty list answers the symbol 'nan, not 0. An average of no numbers has no value, and 0 would be a real answer to an unanswerable question. Test for it with (eq? result 'nan) before doing arithmetic on the result. Until 2026-08-02 it answered 0, which disagreed with its own twin stat/mean's documentation." "A non-list argument raises: (mean 5) raises `mean: expected a list of numbers, got the number 5`. Until 2026-08-02 it answered 0 — the implementation tested the argument's length, a number has none, so the empty branch was taken and a confident wrong number came back." "Every element must be a number; a string or a symbol in the list raises rather than being folded in." "Sensitive to outliers: one very large value pulls the mean away from the bulk of the data. Compare with stat/median when the data may be skewed." "mean takes a LIST, not a vector.")
  :drawbacks ("No weighted mean; multiply and divide by the weight total yourself." "Reports no spread — always show stat/sd or stat/variance beside it." "Recomputes the sum on every call, so a rolling mean over a long series is better served by sma.")
  :usecases ("Report the typical value of a set of measurements." "Centre data before computing deviations, variance, or z-scores." "Compare two groups by their averages.")
  :related ("sum" "stat/mean" "stat/median" "sma" "stat/sd")
  :learn (:concept "Centre: one number that stands in for many; the mean is the balance point of the deviations." :prerequisites ("sum" "list" "/") :progression "Learn mean, then sma for a mean that moves along a series, then stat/sd for how far the data typically sits from the mean.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "Eight request timings came back from a load test and the operator needs the single figure that goes on the report line."
    :eng-program "(define times (list 120 140 100 160))\n(display (list 'n (length times) 'mean (mean times)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (linear? xs a b)\n  (< (abs (- (mean (map (lambda (x) (+ b (* a x))) xs))\n             (+ b (* a (mean xs))))) 1e-9))\n(display (linear? (list 2 4 6) 3 7))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (mean-or xs fallback)\n  (if (null? xs) fallback (mean xs)))\n(display (list (mean-or (list) 'no-data) (mean-or (list 4 6) 'no-data)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: carry the count next to the mean so the reader can judge it. math: shifting and scaling the data shifts and scales the mean by the same amounts — linearity, witnessed. cs: wrap the empty case when you want your own sentinel; the verb's own answer there is 'nan."
    :meta "Reach for mean and NOT sum: sum grows with the number of measurements, so it cannot be compared across runs of different length. The mean can.")
   (:eng "One pass; O(n) and allocation-free." :math "μ = (Σxᵢ)/n." :cs "A fold followed by a division, with an explicit sentinel for the empty case."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'divide a total by the number of things that made it'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (mean (list 5 10 15)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "school — an average grade" :code "(define grades (list 80 90 100))\n(display (list 'average (mean grades)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "monitoring — average latency of a window" :code "(define window (list 30 34 26 30))\n(display (list 'ms (mean window)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Statistics reads it as the first moment. A school reads it as the grade average. Monitoring reads it as expected latency. The arithmetic is identical; the story attached to it is not."
    :meta "Any per-unit rate is a total divided by a count, which is why this shape appears everywhere.")
   (:eng "Same call wherever a per-item figure is wanted." :math "μ estimates E[X] from a sample." :cs "Aggregate-then-normalise — the second half of most reduce pipelines."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "mean is linear: shifting every value by b and scaling by a shifts and scales the mean the same way."
    :eng-program "(define d (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(display (< (abs (- (mean (map (lambda (x) (+ 7 (* 3 x))) d))\n                    (+ 7 (* 3 (mean d))))) 1e-9))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (deviations-cancel? xs)\n  (let ((m (mean xs)))\n    (< (abs (sum (map (lambda (x) (- x m)) xs))) 1e-9)))\n(display (list (deviations-cancel? (list 1 2 3))\n               (deviations-cancel? (list 10 10 40))))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (mean-equals-sum-over-n? xs)\n  (= (mean xs) (/ (sum xs) (length xs))))\n(display (mean-equals-sum-over-n? (list 2 7 9)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ list xs of numbers, a b ∈ ℝ. mean(a·xs + b) = a·mean(xs) + b, and Σ(xᵢ − mean(xs)) = 0"
    :meta "Linear-functional pattern: the mean commutes with affine change of units, which is why changing Celsius to Fahrenheit changes the average by the same formula. Falsified by any a, b where the two sides differ by more than float tolerance.")
   (:eng "Unit changes never require re-averaging the raw data." :math "μ(aX+b) = aμ(X)+b — a defining property of expectation." :cs "Tolerance-based comparison, because doubles do not land exactly."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "mean turns a list of numbers into their balance point."
    :eng-composition "(define (centre xs)\n  (let ((m (mean xs)))\n    (map (lambda (x) (- x m)) xs)))\n(display (centre (list 2 4 6)))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (variance xs)\n  (let ((m (mean xs)))\n    (mean (map (lambda (x) (* (- x m) (- x m))) xs))))\n(display (variance (list 2 4 6)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (group-means groups)\n  (map (lambda (g) (list (car g) (mean (cdr g)))) groups))\n(display (group-means (list (list 'a 1 2 3) (list 'b 10 20))))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A centre-and-spread pipeline emerges: mean once to find the middle, subtract to centre the data, then mean the squared results to get the spread. Two calls to the same verb produce both halves of a distribution's summary."
    :meta "This is the moment family — mean is the first moment, variance the second about the mean; every higher moment reuses the same subtract-then-average move.")
   (:eng "Centred data is what most downstream models expect." :math "Var(X) = E[(X − μ)²] — the mean of the squared deviations." :cs "Two passes: one for μ, one for the deviations.")))

(verb
  :name "sma" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(sma lst n) -> list"
  :summary "Simple moving average: the mean of every consecutive window of n values, in order."
  :explanation "Slide a window of width n along the list and take the mean inside it at each stop. The result is a shorter list — a series of length L becomes a series of length L − n + 1, because the first n − 1 positions have no full window behind them. That shrinkage is the honest part: a moving average cannot report a value it has not yet seen enough data for. Widening n smooths harder and shortens more; narrowing n follows the data more closely and shortens less. This is the verb behind every 20-day average on a price chart and every smoothed sensor trace."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sma (list 1 2 3 4 5) 3)" :note "(2 3 4) — the means of (1 2 3), (2 3 4) and (3 4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(sma (list 10 20 30 40) 2)" :note "(15 25 35) — a window of 2 leaves three windows in a list of four.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(sma (list 1 2 3) 3)" :note "(2) — a window as wide as the list gives exactly one value, the mean of everything.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(sma (list 4 8) 5)" :note "() — a window wider than the list yields nothing; there is never a full window to average.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (smoothing-shrinks? xs n)\n  (= (length (sma xs n)) (- (length xs) (- n 1))))\n(display (list (smoothing-shrinks? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6) 3)\n               (smoothing-shrinks? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6) 1)))\n(newline)" :note "(#t #t) — the output length is always L − n + 1, including the n = 1 case where nothing is smoothed at all.")
  )
  :caveats ("The window must be a positive whole number. (sma (list 1 2 3) 0) raises `sma: expected a positive window width, got the number 0`, and a negative or fractional width raises the same way. Until 2026-08-02 a width of 0 answered (+nan.0 +nan.0 +nan.0 +nan.0) — FOUR entries out of a three-element list, each of them 0 divided by 0." "The list must be a list of numbers; anything else raises rather than being averaged." "The result is SHORTER than the input by n − 1. If you plot it against the original series you must align the two, or every point will be off by n − 1 positions." "There is no leading padding and no partial-window mode: the first n − 1 positions simply have no output." "Each window is summed from scratch, so the cost is O(L·n) rather than the O(L) a running sum would give.")
  :drawbacks ("Unweighted — every value in the window counts the same, so an sma lags a trend by roughly n/2 positions." "No exponential or weighted variant is provided." "Recomputes overlapping sums; slow for large windows on long series.")
  :usecases ("Smooth a noisy sensor or price series before looking for a trend." "Reduce spikes in a metric so a threshold alarm stops flapping." "Compare a fast and a slow average of the same series to spot crossovers.")
  :related ("mean" "stat/mean" "sum" "stat/sd")
  :learn (:concept "Windowed aggregation: applying a summary to every consecutive slice of a sequence rather than to the whole." :prerequisites ("mean" "list" "length") :progression "Learn mean over a whole list, then sma over sliding windows, then compare two sma widths to build a crossover signal.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A temperature sensor reports every second and the raw trace jitters too much to read; the operator wants the shape of the change, not the noise."
    :eng-program "(define trace (list 20 24 19 23 20 22))\n(display (list 'raw (length trace) 'smoothed (sma trace 3)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (shrinkage xs n)\n  (- (length xs) (length (sma xs n))))\n(display (list (shrinkage (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)\n               (shrinkage (list 1 2 3 4 5) 4)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (safe-sma xs n)\n  (if (and (integer? n) (> n 0)) (sma xs n) 'bad-window))\n(display (list (safe-sma (list 1 2 3) 2) (safe-sma (list 1 2 3) 0)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: show the smoothed series next to the raw length so the shrinkage is visible. math: the shrinkage is always n − 1, whatever the data. cs: the verb refuses a width of 0 itself; guard it here only when you would rather carry your own marker than take the error."
    :meta "Reach for sma and NOT mean: mean gives ONE number for the whole series and destroys the trend. sma keeps the series a series and only removes the jitter.")
   (:eng "O(L·n); no state carried between calls." :math "SMAₙ(t) = (1/n)·Σ x from t−n+1 to t." :cs "A sliding-window reduce with no running-sum optimisation."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'summarise every consecutive window of a sequence, not the sequence as a whole'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (sma (list 1 3 5 7 9) 2))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "finance — a price trend" :code "(define closes (list 100 104 102 108 106))\n(display (sma closes 3))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "signal processing — a low-pass filter" :code "(define samples (list 0 8 0 8 0 8))\n(display (sma samples 2))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Statistics reads it as a windowed mean. Finance reads it as a trend line. Signal processing reads it as a box-car low-pass filter. All three are the same convolution with a rectangular kernel of height 1/n."
    :meta "Every moving statistic — moving median, moving max, rolling variance — has this shape; only the summary inside the window changes.")
   (:eng "One verb serves charting, monitoring and filtering." :math "Convolution with a length-n rectangular kernel." :cs "Sliding window — the pattern behind rolling aggregates everywhere."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "The output length is exactly L − n + 1 for 0 < n ≤ L, and a window of width 1 returns the input unchanged."
    :eng-program "(define xs (list 5 10 15 20 25 30))\n(display (list (length (sma xs 2)) (length (sma xs 3)) (length (sma xs 6))))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (length-law? xs n)\n  (= (length (sma xs n)) (+ 1 (- (length xs) n))))\n(display (list (length-law? (list 1 2 3 4 5) 1)\n               (length-law? (list 1 2 3 4 5) 3)\n               (length-law? (list 1 2 3 4 5) 5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (window-1-is-identity? xs)\n  (equal? (sma xs 1) xs))\n(display (window-1-is-identity? (list 3 1 4 1 5)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ list xs of length L, ∀ n with 0 < n ≤ L. length(sma xs n) = L − n + 1, and sma(xs, 1) = xs"
    :meta "Boundary-honesty pattern: the verb never invents a value for a window it does not have. Falsified by any output longer than L − n + 1 — which is exactly what n = 0 used to produce, and why n = 0 now raises instead.")
   (:eng "Knowing the length law is what lets you align the smoothed trace to the raw one." :math "|SMAₙ(x)| = |x| − n + 1 for a valid window." :cs "n = 1 is the identity element of smoothing."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "sma turns a series into a smoother series of windowed means."
    :eng-composition "(define (crossover xs fast slow)\n  (list 'fast (sma xs fast) 'slow (sma xs slow)))\n(display (crossover (list 10 12 11 15 14 18) 2 4))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (smoothed-range xs n)\n  (let ((s (sma xs n)))\n    (- (apply max s) (apply min s))))\n(display (smoothed-range (list 1 9 1 9 1 9) 2))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (double-smooth xs n)\n  (sma (sma xs n) n))\n(display (double-smooth (list 1 2 3 4 5 6) 2))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A two-speed trend detector emerges: run a fast and a slow sma over the same series and watch where they cross. The crossover is the classic buy/sell and alarm/clear signal, built from nothing but two calls to one verb."
    :meta "This is the multi-scale family — the same idea as difference-of-Gaussians in vision and band-pass filtering in audio: subtract a slow smoothing from a fast one and what remains is the change.")
   (:eng "Two widths shrink by different amounts, so align on the shorter." :math "Smoothing twice with width n approximates a triangular kernel of width 2n−1." :cs "sma composes with itself; each pass costs another n−1 of length.")))

(verb
  :name "pct" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(pct a b) -> number"
  :summary "What percentage a is of b: a divided by b, times 100."
  :explanation "A ratio dressed for a human: pct answers 'a is what percent of b'. The first argument is the part, the second is the whole, and the order matters — (pct 3 4) is 75 while (pct 4 3) is about 133. Nothing is clamped, so a part larger than its whole gives more than 100, which is usually what you want when comparing this year to last. pct divides over machine floats rather than over exact rationals, so a ratio that does not divide evenly comes back with a float tail: (pct 1 3) is 33.33333333333333, not the exact 100/3. That is deliberate — a percentage is written to be read, and round2 is the verb that tidies the tail."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(pct 25 200)" :note "12.5 — twenty-five is twelve and a half percent of two hundred.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(pct 3 4)" :note "75 — three quarters expressed the way a report wants it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(pct 200 50)" :note "400 — the part may exceed the whole; pct does not clamp to 100.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(display (list (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (pct 7 0))\n               (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (/ 7 0))))\n(newline)" :note "(raised raised) — a whole of zero raises `pct: division by zero`, the same refusal (/ 7 0) gives. The two verbs agree, which is the point: there is one answer in this language to dividing by nothing, and it is not a number.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (breakdown parts)\n  (let ((total (sum parts)))\n    (map (lambda (p) (pct p total)) parts)))\n(display (breakdown (list 25 25 50)))\n(newline)" :note "(25 25 50) — each part as a percentage of the total; the shares add back to 100.")
  )
  :caveats ("A whole of zero RAISES `pct: division by zero`, exactly as (/ 7 0) does. Until 2026-08-02 it answered 0, which read as 'seven is nought percent of nothing' — a false statement about an undefined question, and a disagreement with / about what dividing by zero means. Guard with zero? when the whole may be zero and you want your own answer instead of the error." "Both arguments must be numbers; a string or a symbol raises rather than being coerced." "The argument order is (part whole). Swapping them produces a plausible-looking wrong number, not an error." "No clamping: values above 100 and below 0 are returned as computed." "Float tails are normal — (pct 1 3) is 33.33333333333333, not a rounded 33.33. Round at the point of display, not in the arithmetic.")
  :drawbacks ("No rounding or formatting; the answer is a raw double, not a display string." "No percentage-point variant — subtracting two pct results gives points, and the verb will not remind you of the difference." "No sentinel for the zero whole: the answer is an error, so a caller that wants to keep going must guard the call itself.")
  :usecases ("Turn a count into a share of a total for a report or a chart." "Express a test pass rate or a completion figure." "Compute each item's slice of a budget.")
  :related ("pct-change" "math/pct" "profit" "fee" "sum")
  :learn (:concept "Ratio as a rate per hundred: the same number a fraction holds, scaled so people can compare it at a glance." :prerequisites ("/" "sum") :progression "Learn pct for 'part of whole', then pct-change for 'how much did it move', then cagr for 'how fast did it move per year'.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A test run finished with 47 passes out of 50 and the report line needs a share, not a pair of raw counts."
    :eng-program "(define passed 47)\n(define total 50)\n(display (list 'pass-rate (pct passed total)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (shares-sum-to-100? parts)\n  (let ((t (sum parts)))\n    (< (abs (- 100 (sum (map (lambda (p) (pct p t)) parts)))) 1e-9)))\n(display (shares-sum-to-100? (list 3 5 12)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (safe-pct a b)\n  (if (= b 0) 'undefined (pct a b)))\n(display (list (safe-pct 7 0) (safe-pct 7 28)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: one share replaces two counts on the report line. math: every part's share of the same total adds back to 100 — the check that a breakdown is complete. cs: guard the zero whole when the pipeline must continue past it, because the verb's own answer there is an error."
    :meta "Reach for pct and NOT pct-change: pct compares a part to a whole at one moment; pct-change compares one moment to another. 47 of 50 is a share; 47 after 50 is a fall.")
   (:eng "One division and one multiplication; no allocation." :math "100·a/b." :cs "A checked divide that signals on a zero denominator rather than inventing a value."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'express a part relative to a whole on a fixed scale'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (pct 12 48))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "engineering — disk used of disk total" :code "(display (list 'used-pct (pct 180 240)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "school — marks scored of marks available" :code "(display (list 'grade (pct 44 50)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Statistics reads it as a relative frequency. Engineering reads it as utilisation. A school reads it as a grade. The scale of 100 is a human convention laid over the same division."
    :meta "Any normalised comparison has this shape; per-mille and parts-per-million differ only in the multiplier.")
   (:eng "Utilisation, coverage and pass rate are all one call." :math "A ratio rescaled by 100." :cs "Normalise-to-a-fixed-range — the same move as mapping to 0..1."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "pct is scale-invariant: multiplying part and whole by the same non-zero factor leaves the percentage unchanged."
    :eng-program "(display (= (pct 3 4) (pct 300 400)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (scale-invariant? a b k)\n  (< (abs (- (pct a b) (pct (* k a) (* k b)))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (scale-invariant? 3 4 100) (scale-invariant? 7 9 0.5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (whole-of-itself? b)\n  (= (pct b b) 100))\n(display (list (whole-of-itself? 5) (whole-of-itself? 1234)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ a, ∀ b ≠ 0, ∀ k ≠ 0. pct(k·a, k·b) = pct(a, b), and pct(b, b) = 100"
    :meta "Scale-invariance pattern: percentages are unitless, which is exactly why they can compare quantities measured in different units. Falsified by any k where the two sides differ.")
   (:eng "Unit changes never change a percentage — that is the point of using one." :math "The ratio a/b is homogeneous of degree 0." :cs "Tolerance comparison; doubles do not land exactly on 75 for every k."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "pct expresses one number as a share of another."
    :eng-composition "(define (utilisation-report used total)\n  (let ((p (pct used total)))\n    (list 'used used 'of total 'pct p 'headroom (- 100 p))))\n(display (utilisation-report 180 240))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (largest-share parts)\n  (let ((t (sum parts)))\n    (apply max (map (lambda (p) (pct p t)) parts))))\n(display (largest-share (list 2 3 5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (over-budget? spent budget limit-pct)\n  (> (pct spent budget) limit-pct))\n(display (list (over-budget? 90 100 80) (over-budget? 50 100 80)))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A utilisation gauge emerges: the share used, and the same share subtracted from 100 as headroom. Two readings of one division, which is what every capacity dashboard puts on screen."
    :meta "This is the budget-and-headroom family — disk quotas, rate limits and burn-down charts all present the used share and its complement together.")
   (:eng "Headroom is the number that decides whether to page someone." :math "share + complement = 100 by construction." :cs "A percentage threshold is the standard shape of an alerting rule.")))

(verb
  :name "pct-change" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(pct-change old new) -> number"
  :summary "The percentage movement from an old value to a new one; negative when it fell."
  :explanation "The old value is the baseline and everything is measured against it: (new − old) / old, times 100. That baseline is why the verb is asymmetric — a fall of 50% and the rise that undoes it are not the same number, because the second one is measured from a smaller base. Read the argument order as chronological: old first, new second. Getting it backwards yields a number that looks reasonable and is wrong, which is worse than an error."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(pct-change 200 250)" :note "25 — two hundred rose to two hundred and fifty, a quarter more.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(pct-change 80 60)" :note "-25 — a fall comes back negative; the sign carries the direction.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(pct-change 50 50)" :note "0 — no movement is a change of zero percent.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(display (guard (e (#t 'no-baseline)) (pct-change 0 9)))\n(newline)" :note "no-baseline — growth from nothing raises `pct-change: division by zero`, because there is no baseline to measure nine against. The guard is how you carry your own word for it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (not-symmetric? a b)\n  (not (= (pct-change a b) (- (pct-change b a)))))\n(display (list (pct-change 100 50) (pct-change 50 100) (not-symmetric? 100 50)))\n(newline)" :note "(-50 100 #t) — halving is −50% but doubling back is +100%; percentage change is not symmetric, and that asymmetry is the most common way people misread it.")
  )
  :caveats ("A ZERO BASELINE RAISES `pct-change: division by zero`, the same refusal / gives. Until 2026-08-02 (pct-change 0 9) answered 0, claiming nothing changed when the value went from nothing to nine — growth from a zero base is undefined, and 0 was a confident false answer to it." "Both arguments must be numbers; anything else raises rather than being coerced." "The argument order is (old new), oldest first. Reversing it gives a wrong number, never an error." "Not symmetric: (pct-change 100 50) is -50 but (pct-change 50 100) is 100. Percentage falls and the rises that undo them never match." "Not additive across steps: two consecutive +10% moves make +21%, not +20%. Chain the values, not the percentages." "A negative baseline produces a sign that is easy to misread; the formula divides by old, so the sign of old flips the sign of the answer.")
  :drawbacks ("No log-return variant, which is the additive alternative for chaining many periods." "No handling for a change of sign across zero, where percentage change stops being meaningful." "Returns a raw double with a float tail; format at display time.")
  :usecases ("Report growth or decline between two measurements." "Compare this month's figure with last month's on a common scale." "Feed a threshold alarm that fires on a movement larger than some percentage.")
  :related ("pct" "cagr" "profit" "sma")
  :learn (:concept "Relative change: measuring a movement against where it started, not in absolute units." :prerequisites ("pct" "-" "/") :progression "Learn pct-change for one step, then cagr for the average step across many years, then sma to smooth a series before measuring change at all.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "Last month's sign-ups were 400, this month's are 460, and the summary needs the movement rather than the two raw numbers."
    :eng-program "(define last-month 400)\n(define this-month 460)\n(display (list 'change (pct-change last-month this-month)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (chaining-is-not-adding? v r1 r2)\n  (let* ((step1 (* v (+ 1 (/ r1 100))))\n         (step2 (* step1 (+ 1 (/ r2 100)))))\n    (list 'compounded (pct-change v step2) 'naive-sum (+ r1 r2))))\n(display (chaining-is-not-adding? 100 10 10))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (safe-change old new)\n  (if (= old 0) 'no-baseline (pct-change old new)))\n(display (list (safe-change 0 9) (safe-change 400 460)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: one signed number replaces two counts. math: compounding two 10% steps gives 21%, not 20% — percentages do not add. cs: guard the zero baseline when the report must still print a row, because the verb's own answer there is an error."
    :meta "Reach for pct-change and NOT pct: pct asks 'what fraction of the whole is this', pct-change asks 'how far has this moved from where it was'. Feeding two dates into pct gives a ratio of the two, not the growth between them.")
   (:eng "One subtraction and one division; the baseline is the second-guessing risk, not the cost." :math "100·(new − old)/old." :cs "A checked divide that signals on a zero baseline rather than inventing a value."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'measure a movement relative to where it started'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (pct-change 40 50))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "finance — a price move" :code "(display (list 'return (pct-change 120 132)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "engineering — a latency regression" :code "(display (list 'regression (pct-change 30 45)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Statistics reads it as relative change. Finance reads it as a return. Performance engineering reads it as a regression. The baseline in the denominator is what all three share and what makes all three asymmetric."
    :meta "Every 'compared to before' figure has this shape; only the sign convention and the name change.")
   (:eng "Same call for growth, return and regression." :math "(x₁ − x₀)/x₀ — the discrete relative derivative." :cs "Delta over baseline — the normalised-difference pattern."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "pct-change and the value it describes are inverses: applying the reported change to the old value reconstructs the new one."
    :eng-program "(define old 400)\n(define new 460)\n(display (< (abs (- new (* old (+ 1 (/ (pct-change old new) 100))))) 1e-9))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (reconstructs? old new)\n  (< (abs (- new (* old (+ 1 (/ (pct-change old new) 100))))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (reconstructs? 200 250) (reconstructs? 80 60) (reconstructs? 7 7)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (asymmetric? a b)\n  (not (= (pct-change a b) (- (pct-change b a)))))\n(display (list (asymmetric? 100 50) (asymmetric? 10 10)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ old ≠ 0, ∀ new. old · (1 + pct-change(old, new)/100) = new"
    :meta "Round-trip pattern: the percentage carries enough information to rebuild the new value from the old, but not the other way round without the old value. Falsified by any pair where the reconstruction misses.")
   (:eng "Reconstruction is the audit check that a reported growth figure is honest." :math "The relative change determines the multiplicative factor 1 + r." :cs "Round-trip testing — encode, decode, compare."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "pct-change reports a single signed movement."
    :eng-composition "(define (series-changes xs)\n  (if (or (null? xs) (null? (cdr xs)))\n      (list)\n      (cons (pct-change (car xs) (cadr xs))\n            (series-changes (cdr xs)))))\n(display (series-changes (list 100 110 99)))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (volatility xs)\n  (let ((changes (if (or (null? xs) (null? (cdr xs)))\n                     (list 0)\n                     (map (lambda (a b) (pct-change a b))\n                          (reverse (cdr (reverse xs)))\n                          (cdr xs)))))\n    (mean (map abs changes))))\n(display (volatility (list 100 110 99 108)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (alert-on-jump xs limit)\n  (if (or (null? xs) (null? (cdr xs)))\n      (list)\n      (let ((c (pct-change (car xs) (cadr xs))))\n        (if (> (abs c) limit)\n            (cons (list 'jump c) (alert-on-jump (cdr xs) limit))\n            (alert-on-jump (cdr xs) limit)))))\n(display (alert-on-jump (list 100 101 140 141) 20))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A returns series emerges: walk a list of levels pairwise and every adjacent pair becomes one percentage move. Averaging the absolute moves gives a crude volatility; thresholding them gives a jump alarm."
    :meta "This is the differencing family — turn a series of levels into a series of changes, which is the first step of almost every time-series method.")
   (:eng "A jump alarm on relative movement survives changes in the absolute scale." :math "Differencing removes a trend and exposes the increments." :cs "Pairwise walk over adjacent elements — one shorter than the input.")))

(verb
  :name "profit" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(profit revenue cost) -> number"
  :summary "What is left when cost is taken out of revenue; negative when cost was larger."
  :explanation "Subtraction with a name. profit is exactly (- revenue cost), and the reason it exists as its own verb is that the name says which argument is which: revenue first, cost second. A loss is a negative profit — the verb does not floor at zero, does not raise, and does not rename the result. That is deliberate: a business that loses money should see the minus sign. Pair it with pct to turn the amount into a margin, and with fee to take a commission out before the subtraction."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(profit 1000 400)" :note "600 — a thousand came in, four hundred went out.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(profit 250 250)" :note "0 — break-even: revenue exactly covered cost.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(profit 30 45)" :note "-15 — a loss comes back negative rather than as an error or a zero.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(profit 1000 (fee 1000 5))" :note "950 — nest fee inside profit to take a five-percent commission off the top.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (margin-pct revenue cost)\n  (pct (profit revenue cost) revenue))\n(display (list (margin-pct 1000 400) (margin-pct 250 250)))\n(newline)" :note "(60 0) — profit gives the amount, pct turns it into the margin percentage.")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument order is (revenue cost). Reversing them returns the loss with the sign flipped and no complaint." "Both arguments must already be in the same currency and the same period; the verb does no conversion." "It computes over machine floats, so money with decimal parts carries float error. Work in whole cents if exactness matters." "The result is a raw number with no unit and no formatting attached.")
  :drawbacks ("Two arguments only; a multi-line cost breakdown must be summed first." "No margin percentage variant — compose with pct." "No tax, discount or currency handling of any kind.")
  :usecases ("Report the bottom line of a sale or a period." "Combine with fee to compute what is left after commission." "Feed the numerator of a margin percentage.")
  :related ("fee" "pct" "sum" "cagr")
  :learn (:concept "Named subtraction: giving an operation a domain name so the argument order documents itself." :prerequisites ("-" "sum") :progression "Learn profit for the amount, then pct for the margin, then cagr for growth of that margin over years.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A market stall took 1000 over the day and spent 400 on stock; the owner wants the one number that says how the day went."
    :eng-program "(define revenue 1000)\n(define cost 400)\n(display (list 'revenue revenue 'cost cost 'profit (profit revenue cost)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (profit-is-subtraction? r c)\n  (= (profit r c) (- r c)))\n(display (list (profit-is-subtraction? 1000 400) (profit-is-subtraction? 30 45)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (classify r c)\n  (let ((p (profit r c)))\n    (cond ((> p 0) 'gain) ((< p 0) 'loss) (else 'break-even))))\n(display (list (classify 10 4) (classify 4 10) (classify 5 5)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: show the inputs beside the answer so the figure can be checked. math: profit is literally subtraction — nothing is hidden. cs: branch on the sign to get the word the human wanted."
    :meta "Reach for profit and NOT pct: profit answers 'how much is left' in currency; pct answers 'what share is that' as a percentage. A margin of 60% and a profit of 600 are different facts about the same day.")
   (:eng "One subtraction; the value of the verb is the argument names." :math "π = R − C." :cs "A two-argument pure function with no failure mode."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'what remains after taking one quantity out of another'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (profit 900 350))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "engineering — headroom left in a budget" :code "(display (list 'ms-left (profit 16 11)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "physics — net force on a body" :code "(display (list 'newtons (profit 40 25)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Business reads it as profit. Frame budgeting reads it as the milliseconds still available. Mechanics reads it as a net force. One subtraction, three vocabularies — and in every one the negative case means 'you went over'."
    :meta "Any net-of-two-quantities figure has this shape; the domain supplies the word for the negative case.")
   (:eng "Overrun always shows as a negative, which is what makes it visible." :math "A difference on the reals; the sign is the whole message." :cs "Net-of pattern — allocate minus used, in minus out."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "profit is antisymmetric in its arguments and additive across separately-reported lines."
    :eng-program "(display (= (profit 30 45) (- (profit 45 30))))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (additive-across-lines? r1 c1 r2 c2)\n  (= (profit (+ r1 r2) (+ c1 c2))\n     (+ (profit r1 c1) (profit r2 c2))))\n(display (list (additive-across-lines? 100 40 200 90)\n               (additive-across-lines? 10 25 5 5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (break-even? r c)\n  (= (profit r c) 0))\n(display (list (break-even? 250 250) (break-even? 250 200)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ r c ∈ ℝ. profit(r, c) = −profit(c, r), and profit(r₁+r₂, c₁+c₂) = profit(r₁,c₁) + profit(r₂,c₂)"
    :meta "Antisymmetry-plus-additivity pattern: additivity is why a per-line profit report can be totalled, and antisymmetry is why swapping the arguments is a silent error rather than a loud one.")
   (:eng "Line-by-line profits total to the whole period's profit." :math "Subtraction is a bilinear form's simplest case." :cs "The additivity is what makes a chunked or parallel roll-up correct."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "profit takes cost out of revenue."
    :eng-composition "(define (net-after-commission revenue cost rate)\n  (profit (profit revenue cost) (fee revenue rate)))\n(display (net-after-commission 1000 400 5))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (total-profit lines)\n  (sum (map (lambda (l) (profit (car l) (cadr l))) lines)))\n(display (total-profit (list (list 100 40) (list 200 90) (list 50 60))))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (profitable-lines lines)\n  (filter (lambda (l) (> (profit (car l) (cadr l)) 0)) lines))\n(display (profitable-lines (list (list 100 40) (list 50 60))))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A profit-and-loss statement emerges: one profit per line, summed for the period, filtered to show which lines carried the business and which dragged on it."
    :meta "This is the roll-up family — per-item figures aggregated into a period total, with the item-level detail kept so the total can be explained.")
   (:eng "Keeping the per-line detail is what makes the total defensible." :math "Σ(rᵢ − cᵢ) = Σrᵢ − Σcᵢ — the additivity from row 4 at work." :cs "map-then-sum, with a filter for the lines that need attention.")))

(verb
  :name "fee" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(fee amount rate-pct) -> number"
  :summary "The amount a percentage rate takes out of a sum: amount times rate, divided by 100."
  :explanation "The other half of pct. Where pct asks 'what percentage is this of that', fee answers 'what does that percentage come to' — you supply the amount and the rate, and you get the money. The rate is given in percent, not as a fraction: 3 means three percent, not three hundred percent. Note that fee returns the CHARGE, not the remainder; subtract it with profit if you want what is left."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(fee 200 3)" :note "6 — three percent of two hundred.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(fee 1000 2.5)" :note "25 — the rate may have a decimal part.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(fee 500 0)" :note "0 — a rate of zero takes nothing.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(profit 1000 (fee 1000 5))" :note "950 — what is left after a five-percent commission.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (fee-and-pct-agree? amount rate)\n  (< (abs (- rate (pct (fee amount rate) amount))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (fee-and-pct-agree? 200 3) (fee-and-pct-agree? 1234 7.5)))\n(newline)" :note "(#t #t) — fee and pct are inverses: the fee expressed as a percentage of the amount gives the rate back.")
  )
  :caveats ("The rate is a PERCENTAGE. (fee 200 3) is 6, not 600 — passing 0.03 where you meant 3 gives a fee a hundred times too small, silently." "fee returns the charge taken, never the amount remaining. Compose with profit for the net." "A negative rate returns a negative fee — a rebate — with no complaint." "Money in doubles carries float error; work in whole cents when exactness matters." "No rounding to a currency's smallest unit; (fee 33 3) is 0.99 but many rates will produce fractions of a cent.")
  :drawbacks ("Flat rate only — no tiers, caps, floors or minimum charges." "No compounding across periods; call it once per period yourself." "No currency, no rounding policy, no formatting.")
  :usecases ("Compute a commission, a platform cut or a transaction charge." "Take a percentage tax or tip out of a bill." "Model a fixed-rate deduction inside a larger calculation.")
  :related ("pct" "profit" "pct-change" "sum")
  :learn (:concept "Applying a rate: turning a percentage back into an amount, the inverse move to computing a percentage." :prerequisites ("pct" "*" "/") :progression "Learn pct to read a rate off two numbers, then fee to apply a rate to one, then profit to subtract the fee from the amount.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "A marketplace takes five percent of every sale and the seller's statement must show both the cut and the payout."
    :eng-program "(define sale 1000)\n(define cut (fee sale 5))\n(display (list 'sale sale 'cut cut 'payout (profit sale cut)))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (round-trips? amount rate)\n  (< (abs (- rate (pct (fee amount rate) amount))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (round-trips? 250 4) (round-trips? 99 12.5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (fee-with-minimum amount rate floor-amount)\n  (max (fee amount rate) floor-amount))\n(display (list (fee-with-minimum 10 5 1) (fee-with-minimum 1000 5 1)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: show the cut and the payout together so the seller can check the arithmetic. math: fee and pct undo each other. cs: a minimum charge is your own wrapper, because the verb has no floor."
    :meta "Reach for fee and NOT pct: pct takes two amounts and returns a rate; fee takes an amount and a rate and returns an amount. Confusing them makes a 5 mean five percent in one place and five pounds in the other.")
   (:eng "One multiplication and one division; the rate's units are the only trap." :math "f = a·r/100." :cs "A pure two-argument function with no failure mode."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'apply a proportional rate to a quantity'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (fee 400 7))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "engineering — a safety allowance on a load" :code "(display (list 'allowance (fee 250 15)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "chemistry — a percentage of a solution by mass" :code "(display (list 'grams (fee 500 2.5)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Commerce reads it as a commission. Structural engineering reads it as a safety margin. Chemistry reads it as a mass fraction. In all three a rate expressed per hundred is turned back into the quantity it describes."
    :meta "Any proportional allocation has this shape; only the name of the rate changes.")
   (:eng "One verb for commission, allowance and fraction." :math "Scalar multiplication by r/100." :cs "Rate application — the inverse of normalisation."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "fee is linear in the amount and inverts pct."
    :eng-program "(display (= (fee 200 3) (+ (fee 120 3) (fee 80 3))))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (linear-in-amount? a b rate)\n  (< (abs (- (fee (+ a b) rate) (+ (fee a rate) (fee b rate)))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (linear-in-amount? 120 80 3) (linear-in-amount? 7 13 12.5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (inverts-pct? amount rate)\n  (< (abs (- rate (pct (fee amount rate) amount))) 1e-9))\n(display (list (inverts-pct? 200 3) (inverts-pct? 640 17)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ a b ∈ ℝ, ∀ r ∈ ℝ. fee(a+b, r) = fee(a, r) + fee(b, r), and for a ≠ 0, pct(fee(a, r), a) = r"
    :meta "Linearity-plus-inverse pattern: linearity is why a fee on a basket equals the sum of the fees on its items, and the pct inverse is the check that the rate you applied is the rate you meant.")
   (:eng "Charging per item and charging the basket give the same total." :math "fee(·, r) is a linear map with slope r/100." :cs "The inverse round-trip is the unit test that catches a rate given as a fraction."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "fee turns a rate and an amount into a charge."
    :eng-composition "(define (settlement sale cost rate)\n  (let* ((cut (fee sale rate))\n         (net (profit (profit sale cost) cut)))\n    (list 'sale sale 'cost cost 'cut cut 'net net)))\n(display (settlement 1000 400 5))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (total-fees amounts rate)\n  (sum (map (lambda (a) (fee a rate)) amounts)))\n(display (list (total-fees (list 100 200 300) 5) (fee 600 5)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (tiered-fee amount)\n  (if (> amount 500) (fee amount 2) (fee amount 5)))\n(display (list (tiered-fee 1000) (tiered-fee 100)))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A settlement line emerges: sale, cost, commission and net, each a named number the seller can check. Adding a size threshold on top gives the tiered pricing the verb itself does not provide."
    :meta "This is the deduction-stack family — gross, then a chain of rate-based deductions, then the net; payroll, invoicing and marketplace payouts all have this shape.")
   (:eng "Every deduction stays visible as its own line, which is what makes a statement auditable." :math "Linearity means per-item fees total to the basket fee." :cs "Tiers are a branch around a linear verb, not a change to it.")))

(verb
  :name "cagr" :std "Motoi"
  :library "stat"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(cagr begin end years) -> number"
  :summary "Compound annual growth rate: the steady yearly percentage that would take begin to end over the given number of years."
  :explanation "Real growth is lumpy; cagr smooths it into the single steady rate that would have reached the same place. The formula is ((end/begin)^(1/years) − 1) × 100 — the nth root of the total growth factor, turned into a percentage. Because it is a root and not an average, cagr is not the mean of the yearly changes: three years of +50%, −50%, +50% do not average to +16.7% of compound growth. That is exactly the mistake cagr exists to prevent."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(cagr 100 400 2)" :note "100 — doubling each year for two years turns 100 into 400, so the rate is 100 percent.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "apprentice" :code "(cagr 500 500 4)" :note "0 — no change over four years is zero percent a year.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(cagr 200 100 1)" :note "-50 — a halving over one year is a rate of minus fifty percent.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(cagr 100 121 2)" :note "10.000000000000009 — the honest answer is 10, but the nth root is a float operation, so a tail comes back. Round at display time.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "master" :code "(define (reconstructs? begin years rate)\n  (let ((factor (expt (+ 1 (/ rate 100)) years)))\n    (* begin factor)))\n(display (reconstructs? 100 2 (cagr 100 400 2)))\n(newline)" :note "400 — applying the reported rate for the reported number of years rebuilds the ending value.")
  )
  :caveats ("A NON-POSITIVE BEGINNING VALUE RAISES. (cagr 0 500 4) raises `cagr: division by zero` — the growth factor end/begin divides by the beginning value, so a beginning of zero is the same fault (/ 500 0) is. (cagr -100 200 2) raises `cagr: expected a positive beginning value, got the number -100`, which is a different fault and says so: the factor exists but its root is not a rate. Until 2026-08-02 both answered 0, claiming no growth where the question has no answer at all." "A NON-POSITIVE NUMBER OF YEARS RAISES TOO. (cagr 100 200 0) raises `cagr: division by zero`, because the exponent is 1/years; a negative span raises `cagr: expected a positive number of years`. Until 2026-08-02 both answered 0." "All three arguments must be numbers; anything else raises rather than being coerced." "The result carries float tails. (cagr 100 121 2) is 10.000000000000009, not 10 — the nth root is computed in doubles." "cagr is NOT the average of the yearly percentage changes. Averaging pct-change figures over-reports growth whenever the changes vary." "An end value of 0 gives exactly -100, which is correct: everything was lost." "The years argument need not be a whole number, but a fractional year makes the 'annual' in the name a stretch.")
  :drawbacks ("Hides all volatility — two wildly different paths with the same endpoints report the same rate." "Says nothing about the shape of the growth, only its endpoints." "No inflation adjustment, no dividend or cash-flow handling; endpoints only.")
  :usecases ("Compare the growth of two things that grew over different numbers of years." "Report a steady headline rate for a series that actually moved unevenly." "Project forward from a beginning value at an observed historical rate.")
  :related ("pct-change" "pct" "profit" "expt" "sma")
  :learn (:concept "Geometric mean growth: the nth root of a total growth factor, which is the right average for things that multiply rather than add." :prerequisites ("pct-change" "expt" "/") :progression "Learn pct-change for a single step, then cagr for the steady rate across many steps, then compare the two on a volatile series to see why the arithmetic mean of returns misleads.")
  :row-2-audit (
   (:problem "Revenue went 100, 180, 130, 400 across three years and the board wants one growth number, not four."
    :eng-program "(display (list 'cagr (cagr 100 400 3) 'naive-mean (mean (list 80 -27.77777777777778 207.69230769230768))))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (geometric-not-arithmetic? begin end years steps)\n  (list 'cagr (cagr begin end years) 'arithmetic-mean (mean steps)))\n(display (geometric-not-arithmetic? 100 100 2 (list 50 -33.33333333333333)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (safe-cagr begin end years)\n  (if (and (> begin 0) (> years 0)) (cagr begin end years) 'undefined))\n(display (list (safe-cagr 0 500 4) (safe-cagr 100 400 2)))\n(newline)"
    :explanation "eng: the compound rate and the naive average of the yearly moves are different numbers, and the naive one is larger. math: a series that returns to where it started has a cagr of 0 while its yearly changes average above 0. cs: guard the non-positive cases when the table must still print a row, because the verb's own answer there is an error."
    :meta "Reach for cagr and NOT (mean of pct-change values): averaging returns adds them, but growth multiplies. The arithmetic mean of returns is always at least the compound rate, and the gap widens with volatility.")
   (:eng "One root and one subtraction; the endpoints are the only inputs." :math "r = (end/begin)^(1/y) − 1, expressed per hundred." :cs "A checked power that signals on a non-positive base or span rather than inventing a rate."))
  :row-3-dimension (
   (:setup "The shape is 'find the constant per-step factor that connects two endpoints across n steps'."
    :rungs (
      (:domain "statistics (this verb)" :code "(display (cagr 100 400 2))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "biology — a population's steady growth rate" :code "(display (list 'per-year-pct (cagr 1000 8000 3)))\n(newline)")
      (:domain "engineering — a steady per-release regression rate" :code "(display (list 'per-release-pct (cagr 100 133.1 3)))\n(newline)"))
    :explanation "Finance reads it as compound annual growth. Population biology reads it as an exponential growth rate. Release engineering reads it as steady drift per version. In every case the answer is the geometric mean of the per-step factors, not the arithmetic mean."
    :meta "Anything that compounds — interest, populations, error accumulation — is averaged with a root, not a sum.")
   (:eng "The same verb wherever change multiplies rather than adds." :math "The geometric mean of the per-step growth factors." :cs "Root-of-a-ratio — the inverse of repeated multiplication."))
  :row-4-proof (
   (:claim "Applying the reported rate for the reported number of years reconstructs the ending value."
    :eng-program "(define r (cagr 100 400 2))\n(display (< (abs (- 400 (* 100 (expt (+ 1 (/ r 100)) 2)))) 1e-6))\n(newline)"
    :math-program "(define (reconstructs? begin end years)\n  (let ((r (cagr begin end years)))\n    (< (abs (- end (* begin (expt (+ 1 (/ r 100)) years)))) 1e-6)))\n(display (list (reconstructs? 100 400 2) (reconstructs? 1000 1331 3) (reconstructs? 200 100 1)))\n(newline)"
    :cs-program "(define (no-change-is-zero? v years)\n  (= (cagr v v years) 0))\n(display (list (no-change-is-zero? 500 4) (no-change-is-zero? 17 9)))\n(newline)"
    :invariant "∀ begin > 0, ∀ end > 0, ∀ years > 0. begin · (1 + cagr(begin, end, years)/100)^years = end, within float tolerance"
    :meta "Round-trip pattern: the rate carries exactly the information needed to rebuild the endpoint, and nothing about the path taken between them. Falsified by any triple where the reconstruction misses by more than tolerance.")
   (:eng "Reconstruction is the audit check on any published growth rate." :math "The definition rearranged; the root and the power cancel." :cs "Tolerance-based, because roots and powers of doubles do not round-trip exactly."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   (:base "cagr collapses a growth path to one steady rate."
    :eng-composition "(define (project begin rate years)\n  (* begin (expt (+ 1 (/ rate 100)) years)))\n(define observed (cagr 100 400 2))\n(display (list 'in-4-years (project 100 observed 4)))\n(newline)"
    :math-composition "(define (rank-by-growth rows)\n  (sort (map (lambda (r) (list (car r) (cagr (cadr r) (caddr r) (cadddr r)))) rows)\n        (lambda (a b) (> (cadr a) (cadr b)))))\n(display (rank-by-growth (list (list 'a 100 200 5) (list 'b 100 400 5))))\n(newline)"
    :cs-composition "(define (volatility-gap levels years)\n  (let ((steps (map (lambda (a b) (pct-change a b))\n                    (reverse (cdr (reverse levels)))\n                    (cdr levels))))\n    (list 'compound (cagr (car levels) (car (reverse levels)) years)\n          'arithmetic (mean steps))))\n(display (volatility-gap (list 100 150 100) 2))\n(newline)"
    :emergent "A comparison-and-projection table emerges: measure each candidate's steady rate, rank them on a common footing however many years each ran, and project the winner forward. The gap between the compound rate and the arithmetic mean of the steps is itself a volatility reading."
    :meta "This is the normalise-then-compare family — put unlike things on one scale before ranking them; the same move as rate-per-hour, price-per-kilo, or score-per-attempt.")
   (:eng "Ranking on cagr compares a two-year run against a ten-year one fairly." :math "The gap between arithmetic and geometric means grows with variance." :cs "Sort on a derived key — compute once per row, then order.")))
